  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front moves north through the region \nthis morning, followed by a strong cold front this afternoon and\nevening. \n\nLow pressure over the Great Lakes lifts northeast into the\nProvince of Ontario by daybreak, then into the Province of\nQuebec as the day progresses. A warm front south of the area\nlifts north through the region this morning, then low pressure\npassing north of northern New England will drag a cold front \nthrough the region this afternoon and evening.\n\nSome light showers will impact the area through daybreak or so,\npossibly into the late morning with the passage of that warm\nfront. Clouds over the area will lift and dissipate somewhat\nthis afternoon, and then it will warm up quite nicely with highs\nin the upper 70s to low 80s. With clouds breaking up and\nsunshine developing, gusty southwest winds will usher a more\nhumid airmass into the area with surface dew points in the mid\nand upper 60s.\n\nWhile instability values will be relatively low, generally \nSBCAPE values and MLCAPE values around 500 J/kg, but 06-km Bulk \nShear will be upwards of 55-60 kt. SPC has the region in a \nSlight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe weather this afternoon\nand evening. Timing looks to be around 4pm to 6pm for the \nsouthern Poconos, far northwest New Jersey, and the Lehigh \nValley, 5pm to 7pm along the I-95 corridor, and from 6pm to 8 pm\nover Delmarva and southern New Jersey. After sunset, with loss\nof diurnal heating, the risk for severe weather will diminish.\n\nA tight pressure gradient then develops over the area tonight\nthrough Saturday as broad high pressure centered over the\nSouthern Plains begins to build east. Gusty north to northwest\nwinds will develop tonight and will continue through Saturday,\nwith winds ranging from 15 to 25 mph with 25 to 35 mph gusts.\nMuch cooler and drier on Saturday with highs in the 50s and\nsurface dew points in the 30s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread frost and freeze conditions are \nincreasingly likely Saturday night.\n\nThe primary trough axis looks to pass over the region by \nSaturday evening, with subtle height falls through Saturday \nnight and into Sunday. Guidance is coming into better agreement \nthat a portion of the trough will close off and linger offshore \nin the vicinity of the Carolinas, but high pressure will be \nsettling into the region from the northwest. The end result \nshould be mostly clear skies and diminishing winds through \nSaturday night. \n\nWith the coldest airmass so far this season settling into the \nregion, the primary question remaining for Saturday night's \ntemperatures is how quickly winds diminish. Current indications \nare that it could remain a bit breezy through much of the \nevening, shortening the window of good radiational cooling \nconditions. Nevertheless, by dawn Sunday winds should be fairly \nlight at least across the Poconos, Lehigh Valley, and adjacent \nnorthern NJ. Given this, the current forecast calls for \ntemperatures at or below freezing across these areas. For \nsoutheastern PA and central and southern portions of inland NJ, \ntemperatures will likely still fall into the mid 30s. Upper 30s \nto low 40s are expected across much of Delmarva and coastal NJ. \n\nRegarding frost, the airmass that will be infiltrating the area\nwill also be fairly dry, so good radiational cooling will be \nneeded to get widespread frost. Given this, the potential for \nwidespread frost appears limited at this time to the Poconos and\nfar northern NJ, with patchy frost still possible across the \nremainder of our eastern PA counties and inland NJ, especially \nthe Pine Barrens. Frost and/or freeze headlines are still very \nlikely to be needed for at least parts of the area Saturday \nnight. \n\nTemperatures will begin slowly moderating Sunday into the early\npart of next week.",
